INT. DR. FEINBERG'S DENTAL OFFICE - WAITING ROOM - EVENING

Beige chairs, a fish tank, a wall of laminated posters of cheerful molars. The lights are dim. A banner above the receptionist's desk reads, in glitter: SURPRISE, GRANDMA!

Six conspirators crouch behind furniture. GLENDA PRZYBYLSKI, 40s, in a party hat, ducks behind the water cooler holding a cake; her husband MARVIN, 50s, behind the ficus with a stack of paper cups, while their teenage daughter SKYLER, 16, hides under the receptionist's desk, filming on her phone; LAZLO KERESZTES, 30s, the nervous nephew, slips behind the coat stand with a party horn, and THADDEUS FEINBERG, 60s, the dentist, in scrubs and a pointy hat, sits on the reception counter, stifling a laugh, while everyone whispers, shushes, and knocks into everyone else.

The front door chimes.

Everyone freezes. A chair creaks. Lazlo's horn squeaks. Marvin drops a paper cup. Glenda hisses at both of them. Skyler's phone beeps. The door opens. Nobody enters.

THADDEUS
(whispering)
Where is she?

GLENDA
(whispering back)
She's coming.

MARVIN
(whispering)
My knee is stuck.

LAZLO
(whispering)
Mine too.

The door chimes again. Nobody enters. Wind moves a magazine.

GLENDA
Well, that's the worst possible timing.

LAZLO
It's okay. She'll be here. She always is.

The lights flip on. Everyone stands. In the doorway, in a flowered coat, is GRANDMA ROSEMARY, 80s, who looks at the banner, the cake, the party hats, and the open mouth of the dentist, and says, quite calmly:

GRANDMA ROSEMARY
I thought the appointment was tomorrow.
